---
scope: src/tools/upgrade/buildVersionsMeta
type: plan
intent: >
  Rewrite buildVersionsMeta from a runtime git-log helper (docs-only) into a
  build-time script that generates a static versions.json manifest covering
  every deliverable artifact category, shipped inside the published npm package.
---

## Plan

### Step 1: Create the build script enumerateArtifacts helper

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`, `coding-playbook/architecture/folder-structure`

Create `src/tools/upgrade/buildVersionsMeta/enumerateArtifacts/index.ts`.

This helper returns a flat array of `{ slug: string; repoPath: string }` entries
covering every deliverable artifact across all categories. The slug is the
namespaced key that will appear in versions.json. The repoPath is the
repo-relative path used in the `git log` command.

Source registries to enumerate (all imported from existing modules):
- `listMethodologyDocs()` from `@/tools/init/initContent/index.js` — slug pattern: `docs/<hostFilename without .md>`, repoPath: `.aide/docs/<hostFilename>`
- `COMMANDS` from `@/tools/init/scaffoldCommands/index.js` — slug pattern: `commands/<canonical>`, repoPath: use `DOC_PATHS[canonical]` (must be exported or re-derived). Since `DOC_PATHS` is private to initContent, the repoPath must be derived: the canonical name in COMMANDS already maps to a repo path via `readCanonicalDoc` internals, but we need the path not the content. Add a new export `getCanonicalPath(name: CanonicalDocName): string` to initContent that returns the repo-relative path from `DOC_PATHS`. This avoids duplicating the path registry.
- `listAgents()` from `@/tools/init/initContent/index.js` — slug pattern: `agents/<canonical>`, repoPath via `getCanonicalPath()`
- `listSkills()` from `@/tools/init/initContent/index.js` — slug pattern: `skills/<canonical>`, repoPath via `getCanonicalPath()`
- `bin/aide-tree` — slug: `bin/aide-tree`, repoPath: `.aide/bin/aide-tree.mjs`
- Extensions (vscode vsix) — slug: `extensions/vscode/aide-markdown`, repoPath: `extensions/vscode/aide-markdown-0.0.1.vsix`. Note: this is a binary file but git log still tracks it.

Interface to export:
```
export interface ArtifactEntry { slug: string; repoPath: string; }
```

Default export: `enumerateArtifacts(): ArtifactEntry[]` (synchronous, no I/O).

Sub-step 1a: Add `getCanonicalPath` export to `src/tools/init/initContent/index.ts`. This is a one-line function that indexes into `DOC_PATHS` and returns the value. It must be added in this same step because `enumerateArtifacts` depends on it.

Sub-step 1b: Create the `enumerateArtifacts` helper as described above.

### Step 2: Rewrite buildVersionsMeta as a build-time script

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`, `coding-playbook/workflow/testing`

Rewrite `src/tools/upgrade/buildVersionsMeta/index.ts` to be a build-time
script (invoked by `tsx` from a package.json script), not an importable
runtime helper.

The rewritten module:
- Imports `enumerateArtifacts` from `./enumerateArtifacts/index.js`
- Resolves `REPO_ROOT` the same way as before (4-hop walk from `import.meta.url`)
- For each artifact entry, runs `git log --follow --format="%H %aI" -n 2 -- <repoPath>` with `cwd: REPO_ROOT`
- Parses stdout into `VersionMeta` (same shape: `publishedAt`, `sourceCommit`, `previousCommit?`)
- Assembles a `VersionsMap` keyed by the namespaced slug
- Writes the result to a well-known static location: `src/versions.json` (under REPO_ROOT). This file will be compiled into `dist/versions.json` by the TypeScript build step since `src/` is the source root. Actually, JSON files are not compiled by tsc — instead write directly to `dist/versions.json` so it ships with the build output. The `prepublishOnly` script already runs `rm -rf dist && npm run build` first, so the build script runs after tsc.
- On any fatal error (git unavailable, etc.), exits with a non-zero code so the publish pipeline halts — silent failure is an undesired outcome per the spec.

The module should also export the types (`VersionMeta`, `VersionsMap`) and a
`readVersionsManifest()` function that reads the static `dist/versions.json`
(or the co-located `versions.json` resolved via `import.meta.url`) at runtime.
This is the function that `aide_init` and `aide_upgrade` will call instead of
the old `buildVersionsMeta()`.

Restructure the file so:
- Types and `readVersionsManifest()` are named exports (used at runtime by upgrade/init)
- The build-time script logic is in an `async function main()` called only when the module is executed directly (use a top-level `main()` call guarded by detecting direct execution, or simply always run it since this file is only invoked via `tsx scripts/...`)

Actually, cleaner approach: split into two files.
- Keep `src/tools/upgrade/buildVersionsMeta/index.ts` as the runtime interface: exports types (`VersionMeta`, `VersionsMap`) and `readVersionsManifest()` which reads the static JSON file.
- Create `scripts/build-versions.ts` as the build-time entry point: imports `enumerateArtifacts`, runs git log for each, writes `dist/versions.json`.

This separation is cleaner because the build script has different concerns (writes files, exits on error) than the runtime reader (reads a static file).

### Step 3: Create the build script at scripts/build-versions.ts

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`, `coding-playbook/architecture/folder-structure`

Create `scripts/build-versions.ts` — the build-time entry point that
generates `dist/versions.json`.

This script:
- Imports `enumerateArtifacts` from `@/tools/upgrade/buildVersionsMeta/enumerateArtifacts/index.js` (use the path alias — tsx respects tsconfig paths)
- Resolves `REPO_ROOT` as `process.cwd()` (the script is always run from the repo root via package.json)
- Iterates all artifact entries from `enumerateArtifacts()`
- For each, runs `git log --follow --format="%H %aI" -n 2 -- <repoPath>` via `execFile`
- Parses into `VersionMeta` entries (same logic as old buildVersionsMeta)
- Skips entries with no git history (uncommitted files)
- Writes `JSON.stringify(result, null, 2) + "\n"` to `dist/versions.json`
- Logs a summary: `"versions.json: <N> artifacts"` to stderr
- Exits non-zero if: git is completely unavailable, or zero artifacts were resolved (empty manifest is a spec-undesired outcome)

Import `VersionMeta` and `VersionsMap` types from `@/tools/upgrade/buildVersionsMeta/index.js`.

### Step 4: Rewrite buildVersionsMeta/index.ts as the runtime reader

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`, `coding-playbook/architecture/data-flow`

Rewrite `src/tools/upgrade/buildVersionsMeta/index.ts` to be a runtime reader
of the static manifest, not a git-log executor.

The rewritten module:
- Exports `VersionMeta` interface (same shape as before)
- Exports `VersionsMap` type (same as before)
- Default export: `readVersionsManifest(): VersionsMap` — synchronous function that reads `versions.json` from the package's dist directory. Resolve the path relative to `import.meta.url` (the same 4-hop pattern gets to repo root, then `dist/versions.json`; or more directly, since in dist this file lives at `dist/tools/upgrade/buildVersionsMeta/index.js`, walk up 4 levels to reach `dist/`, then read `versions.json` from there).
- Uses `readFileSync` + `JSON.parse`. Wraps in try/catch — if the file is missing (dev environment, not built yet), returns `{}` with a console.warn.
- No dependency on `node:child_process` or git whatsoever.

### Step 5: Wire the static manifest into upgrade and init

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`, `coding-playbook/architecture/folder-structure`

Update consumers to use the static manifest reader.

5a: In `src/tools/upgrade/index.ts` — the import already exists (`import buildVersionsMeta from "./buildVersionsMeta/index.js"`). The function name changes from async `buildVersionsMeta()` to sync `readVersionsManifest()`. Update the import to `import readVersionsManifest from "./buildVersionsMeta/index.js"` and change the call from `await buildVersionsMeta()` to `readVersionsManifest()` (no await needed since it's sync). The rest of phase c (version metadata) stays the same — it serializes the map and compares against the host file.

5b: In `src/tools/init/index.ts` — init currently does NOT write versions.json. Add a new step after `installMethodologyDocs` that reads the manifest via `readVersionsManifest` (imported from `@/tools/upgrade/buildVersionsMeta/index.js`) and includes a planning step to write `versions.json` into the host's doc hub directory. Create an `InitStep` with category `"methodology"`, name `"versions.json"`, status `"would-create"` or `"exists"` depending on whether the file already exists at the target path, and content `JSON.stringify(manifest, null, 2) + "\n"`.

### Step 6: Update package.json build pipeline

Read: `coding-playbook/architecture/folder-structure`

Add a `build:versions` script and wire it into both the regular `build` script and `prepublishOnly`.

- Add `"build:versions": "tsx scripts/build-versions.ts"` to `scripts`
- Change `"build"` to include `build:versions` after tsc: `"build": "tsc && npm run build:versions"` — so every `npm run build` (local dev included) generates `dist/versions.json`. This ensures the local dev server always has a valid manifest without needing the publish pipeline.
- `prepublishOnly` stays as `"rm -rf dist && npm run build"` — since `build` now includes `build:versions`, the publish pipeline gets it automatically.

